---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const APP = "web/app/src";
const ROUTER = "web/app/src/router.tsx";
const TESTS = "server/tests/test_web_app.py";

const H = {
  short: "How a URL becomes a page",
  how: "Routes, loaders and the frame around them",
  example: "A plasmid's page, read before it's drawn",
  rules: "Routes the server serves",
  ref: "Code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "example", label: H.example },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      TanStack Router, with every route written in code in one file. A route's loader reads what
      its page needs through the query cache before the page renders; most pages are split out
      and loaded when they're needed, and links preload on intent. Every path the router has is
      also a Django route that serves the same shell page, and a test reads the route tree to
      check that each one answers 200 (<Adr n={[29, 176]} />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <p>
      The root route's loader reads who's signed in and the role names every page shows, before
      the first render (<Path p={ROUTER} sym="createRootRouteWithContext" />). Under it, each
      route names its parent, its path, any search parameters it accepts, and a loader. Loaders
      read through TanStack Query, which does the caching, so the router keeps no cache of its own
      (<code>defaultPreloadStaleTime: 0</code>). Hovering or focusing a link preloads the route's
      code and its loader's reads (<Path p={ROUTER} sym='defaultPreload: "intent"' />). Paths always
      end in a slash, as Django's do.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pages are split out with <code>lazyRouteComponent</code>, so a person who never opens the
      compute pages never downloads them. How the reads themselves behave, and why they're read
      before a page draws, is <PageLink to="Reading data" />.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every page sits in the same frame: a skip link, the sidebar, the top bar and the page, plus
      the ⌘K search palette (<Path p={`${APP}/layout.tsx`} sym="export function Layout(" />). The
      sidebar reads the same navigation file as the server-rendered pages, so both kinds of page
      list the same sections (<Adr n="0176" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/web/navigation.json" />,{" "}
      <Path p="server/src/iki_server/web/navigation.py" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={3} title={H.example}>
    <p>
      A scientist opens a plasmid's map tab. Django serves the shell for the path, the router
      matches the entity route, and its loader reads, in parallel, the record, the installed types
      and the installed components. Once the record is in, it reads whether the reader watches it,
      because the watch button arriving later would move the others along. An HRID that several
      of the reader's projects have a record of answers with a choice of project rather than an
      error (<Adr n="0115" />):
    </p>
    <Excerpt file={ROUTER} start="^const entityRoute = createRoute\(\{" />
    <p>
      The child route for <code>components/$</code> takes the rest of the path, since a
      component's name is <code>namespace/plugin.component</code>. Its page is split out, and it
      renders the plugin's component in a frame: <PageLink to="Plugin frames" /> follows it from
      there.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Every routed page is served" open>
        <p>
          The app routes in the browser, but a person can open any of its addresses directly, or
          reload one. So every path the router has is also a Django route that serves the shell
          page (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/urls.py" />). A test reads the route tree out of{" "}
          <code>router.tsx</code>, fills each parameter with a sample, and checks every page answers
          200 from Django, so a page added to the router can't be missing from the server's (<Path
            p={TESTS}
            sym="def test_every_page_the_app_routes_loads_it("
          />). Another checks that each sidebar item is a page, and that the app routes those the
          navigation file says it does (<Path
            p={TESTS}
            sym="def test_the_sidebars_items_are_pages_and_the_app_routes_those_it_says("
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="What Django serves on those routes">
        <p>
          The same template for every app path, with no data in it, after the sign-in check: <PageLink
            to="Serving and security policy"
          /> has what's in it and the policy it carries.
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p={ROUTER} />: every route and loader</li>
        <li><Path p={`${APP}/layout.tsx`} />: the frame around every page</li>
        <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/urls.py" />: the server's routes, the app's among them</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0029" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0115" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0176" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
